  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Let $C$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients whose degree is less than or equal to $2.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $g(x)\\in C,$ where $g(x)={x}^{3}$\n- For every $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)\\in C.$\n- The set is closed under addition.",
  "worked_solution": "Let's examine our statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $C$ contains only polynomials whose degree is less than or equal to $2,$ however\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}\u2061({x}^{3})=3>2.\n$$\n\nSo, $g(x)={x}^{3}\\notin C.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ then $\\operatorname{deg}\u2061(p(x))\\le 2$ and $\\operatorname{deg}\u2061(q(x))\\le 2.$ Since $\\operatorname{deg}\u2061(p+q)$ is less than or equal to the maximum of the degrees of $p$ and $q$, we deduce that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}\u2061(p+q)\\le 2.\n$$\n\nHence, $p(x)+q(x)\\in C.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set is closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
